{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"brashy-alkylic-guillemot","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n7-day store mining (2026-08-07..14) found 33 FORCE_CLOSE audit records, of which ~25 are ADMINISTRATIVE closes — duplicate, obsolete, wontfix, stale-premise — where completing the described work is not the intent, so the completion verifier can never PASS them and operators reach for --force. A narrow attested-disposition path already exists ({duplicate, not_a_bug, escalated} + replacement link, observed on 9b70) but (a) it covers too few dispositions and (b) the completion-FAIL message never mentions it: \"the failure message sent the reader the wrong way. It offered only 'complete the work' or '[operator-attested]' — attesting would have been a FALSE claim\" (9b70). This is the single largest FORCE_CLOSE class — larger than all gate-pathology classes combined.\n\n## Fix\n1. Extend the sanctioned disposition classes to cover the observed administrative population: duplicate, not_a_bug, escalated (existing) + obsolete (premise no longer holds), superseded (replaced by named ticket), wontfix (deliberate decision, reason required). Each requires its structured payload (e.g. superseded/duplicate -> replacement ticket link; obsolete/wontfix -> reason text) so the close is attested by DISPOSITION, not by falsely claiming completion.\n2. Disposition closes skip completion verification (there is no work to verify) but are recorded as typed audit events distinct from FORCE_CLOSE, and still respect the signing model (disposition attestation, not completion attestation).\n3. Discoverability — the completion-FAIL / close-refusal message enumerates the disposition path whenever it applies: alongside \"complete the work\" and the (external-evidence-only) attestation path, name `transition --disposition <class>` with the class list. The 9b70 incident is the RED oracle: a wontfix-shaped ticket's failure message must present a truthful exit.\n4. Migration note: existing FORCE_CLOSE records stay as-is (permanent audit); new administrative closes should have no reason to use --force.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] The six disposition classes close a ticket without completion verification, each demanding its required payload (missing payload = refusal); RED->GREEN per class.\n- [ ] A disposition close writes a typed disposition audit event + disposition attestation (never a completion attestation), distinguishable from FORCE_CLOSE in the audit trail; store-event test.\n- [ ] The close-gate FAIL/refusal message enumerates the disposition path with its classes when the ticket is open/in_progress; message test asserts the 9b70 scenario (wontfix-shaped ticket) is offered a truthful exit.\n- [ ] --force on an administrative close logs a hint naming the disposition alternative; test.\n- [ ] docs (ticket-model / user-guide / plan-review-gate) document the disposition path; CLI reference regenerated if flags change.\n- [ ] `make lint`/`make typecheck` clean; lands via Gerrit (LLM-Review +1, Verified +1).","id":"fc20-2df1-426e-42ab","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Sanctioned administrative-disposition close path (duplicate/obsolete/superseded/wontfix/...) discoverable from the close-gate failure message"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786743691433517001,"uuid":"e45faea8-f587-4929-92e9-40d5b6cf492c"}